/**
\page org_mitk_views_tensorreconstruction Tensor Reconstruction

The tensor view reconstructs diffusion tensors from a diffusion-weighted image (DWI). Select the DWI as "Diffusion-weighted Image", choose the method under "Advanced Settings" and press "Start Reconstruction":
\li <b>ITK Linear Least Squares:</b> a linear least-squares fit of the log-signal. Every volume is fitted with its own b-value, so DWIs with several shells are fitted correctly. "Ignore voxels with negative eigenvalues" leaves voxels whose tensor has a negative eigenvalue empty.
\li <b>With correction for negative eigenvalues:</b> the fit is repeated after smoothing the signal of voxels whose tensor has a negative eigenvalue; voxels that still have one are left empty.

Volumes whose b-value rounds to 0 are the baseline volumes; their mean is the non-diffusion weighted signal of a voxel. Voxels whose mean baseline signal is below the "B0 Threshold" are not reconstructed; while the threshold is changed, the excluded voxels are shown as an overlay on the DWI.

The reconstruction runs in the background: the application stays usable, and the buttons of the view are disabled until the result appears as a new node below the DWI (depending on the image size and method, after a few seconds to minutes). The same holds for the images generated from tensors below. A running computation cannot be stopped: closing the view lets it run to its end and drops its result, and quitting the application waits until it has ended.

\imageMacro{tensor4.png,"Tensor image after reconstruction",16.00}

The view also generates images from a tensor image:
\li <b>ODF Image from Tensors:</b> the ODFs are initialized directly from the tensor values and normalized.
\li <b>Diffusion-weighted Image from Tensors:</b> a DWI with the chosen b-value and number of gradient directions (the directions are subdivisions of an icosahedron) and one baseline volume. The baseline signal is the l2-norm of the tensor; the diffusion-weighted volumes follow the tensor model.

\section TensorReconstructionUse When to Use Tensors

The tensor is the simplest model: one ellipsoid per voxel, whose principal axis is the main fiber direction and whose shape gives the FA (\ref org_mitk_views_diffusionquantification). It needs only six or more gradient directions at one b-value (typically 1000 s/mm^2), is robust to noise and is the basis of FA maps, of the colour-coded FA map and of deterministic tractography of compact bundles. It cannot represent crossing fibers: where bundles cross, the tensor is flat (low FA, no clear direction), and tractography stops or turns into the wrong bundle. For such regions, reconstruct Q-ball ODFs and peaks (\ref org_mitk_views_qballreconstruction, \ref org_mitk_views_odfmaximaextraction); the choice is discussed in "Choosing the Model and the Method" of the "Streamline Tractography" manual page.

The tensor image renders as a colour-coded FA map in the 2D views: each voxel gets the colour of its principal direction (red left-right, green anterior-posterior, blue superior-inferior) scaled by its FA. This is the map on which the ROIs of a tract are placed.

\section TensorReconstructionRest REST Endpoints

\li POST /api/v1/diffusion/reconstruction/tensors with {dwi: uid, fit: "lls" or "eigenvalue_correction", b0_threshold, zero_negative_eigenvalues}: the reconstruction of this view as a job; the tensor image is added below the DWI, named as the view names it.
\li POST /api/v1/diffusion/reconstruction/color-fa with {tensors: uid}: the colour-coded FA map as an RGB image below the tensor image, for screenshots.
\li The scalar maps (FA, MD, ...) of a tensor image: POST /api/v1/diffusion/reconstruction/tensor-indices, see \ref org_mitk_views_diffusionquantification.

*/
